  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nThe Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem requires minimizing the maximum overlap of a step function with its time-shifted complement over all possible shifts. Previous approaches used gradient descent and binary search, but these struggled to escape local optima. This solution adopts a genetic algorithm (GA) strategy to explore diverse binary representations of step functions. The GA evolves candidate solutions through crossover and mutation while maintaining the integral constraint. By mapping the optimization to a binary space and applying tournament selection and two-point crossover, the GA can efficiently explore the solution space to find a low-overlap configuration.\n\nMathematical specification\n- Input representation: Binary vectors of length n_points=512 with exactly 512/2 ones\n- Objective: $min \\max_k \\iint h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx$ where $h$ is the step function\n- Constraints: $0 \\le h(x) \\le 1$ and $\\int_0^2 h(x)dx=1$\n- Optimization: GA with tournament selection, two-point crossover (70% probability) and swap mutation (10% probability)\n- Evaluation: Overlap computed using linear correlation with FFT for efficiency\n\nImplementation procedure\n1. Initialization:\n   - n_points=512\n   - Use provided parent solution (if available) and resample to 512 points\n   - Generate population of 40 binary vectors with exactly 256 ones\n\n2. Core loop:\n   - Evaluate fitness (overlap score) for population\n   - Tournament selection (tournament size=3) for parents\n   - Apply crossover (70%) or mutation (10%) to generate next generation\n   - Maintain population size of 40\n   - If time budget exceeded, harvest best candidate\n\n3. Fitness evaluation:\n   - Overlap = np.correlate(binary_vector, 1-binary_vector, mode='full').max() * (2/512)\n   - Compute efficiently using built-in correlation\n\nSearch choices and useful variations\n- Population size: 40 (balance diversity and computational cost)\n- Tournament size: 3 (promotes selective pressure)\n- Crossover rate: 70% (high enough for exploration)\n- Mutation rate: 10% (ensures diversity)\n- Resolution: Fixed at 512 points (trade-off based on time constraints)\n\nNumerical robustness and memory use\n- Memory: Storing population of 40\u00d7512=20480 doubles, negligible for modern systems\n- Computation: Each evaluation uses built-in correlation (O(n^2) but n=512 is concise)\n- Fallback: Binary step function (all constraints satisfied by construction)\n\nRuntime safeguards and return contract\n- Time tracking within run() function\n- Terminate when budget (1100 s) exceeds\n- Return best binary vector and its overlap score (represented as (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n- Overlap computed using np.correlate with linear correlation\n- Candidate repair: Swap mutation maintains integral constraint\n\nThe algorithm produces a step function (binary vector) with disjoint coordinates in a way that minimizes cross-shift overlap. Each candidate is a 512-point binary vector with exactly 256 ones, ensuring proper scaling in [0,2]. The evaluation uses standard linear correlation to compute overlap with all temporal shifts, maintained efficient by the built-in numpy correlate function.\n\nThe solution leverages GA's population-based approach to navigate the non-convex landscape, offering advantages over gradient-based methods. The high crossover rate encourages exploration while appropriate mutation maintains diversity. The fixed resolution of 512 balances representational fidelity and computational tractability within available time.\n\nThis implementation stands robust, satisfying all mathematical requirements and numerical constraints.",
